Chapter: Tls13ScheduleTest
  cites Foreword chapter Aes
  cites Foreword chapter Tls

 The TLS 1.3 key schedule against RFC 8448, "Example Handshake Traces
 for TLS 1.3", section 3. Every value below is one the IETF printed. The
 chapter is not being checked against itself: it is being checked against
 a trace the working group published precisely so implementations could
 not quietly diverge.

 This is the gate on the key schedule. The chapter used to carry two key
 schedules, both wrong. One defined its own tls-hkdf-expand as a single
 HMAC block and silently returned short above 32 bytes. The other
 computed the early secret from a ONE-byte zero IKM where RFC 8446 means
 Hash.length zero bytes. Either bug produces bytes that look like keys
 and decrypt nothing, and neither is visible to a test that only asks
 whether the code agrees with itself.

Section: RFC 8448 Vectors

  x-ecdhe : List Integer =
    [139, 212, 5, 79, 181, 91, 157, 99, 253, 251, 172, 249, 240, 75, 159, 13,
     53, 230, 214, 63, 83, 117, 99, 239, 212, 98, 114, 144, 15, 137, 73, 45]

  x-transcript : List Integer =
    [134, 12, 6, 237, 192, 120, 88, 238, 142, 120, 240, 231, 66, 140, 88, 237,
     214, 180, 63, 44, 163, 230, 233, 95, 2, 237, 6, 60, 240, 225, 202, 216]

Section: Hex

  x-hex : List Integer -> Text
  x-hex (bs) = x-hex-loop bs 0 (list-length bs) ""

  x-hex-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  x-hex-loop (bs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else x-hex-loop bs (i + 1) n (acc & aes-hex-byte (list-at bs i))

Section: The Ladder

 Each line is one rung of RFC 8446 section 7.1, and each is checked
 independently. A schedule that is wrong at the first rung is wrong at
 every rung after it, so testing only the final key would tell us that
 something broke without telling us where.

  test-hash : Text =
    "tls13 hash-empty=" & x-hex (tls13-hash [])

 The trap, pinned. Codex Text is CCE, so a bare char-code gives the CCE
 code point and not the byte the wire wants. For "derived" that is
 160d1511210d16 where the RFC means 6465726976656420 minus the trailing
 space -- 64657269766564. Every key-schedule label goes through this, so
 one mis-encoded label poisons every secret below the first rung while
 still producing 32 confident-looking bytes. This line exists so that if
 anyone ever "simplifies" tls13-ascii back to char-code, the diff is loud.

  test-label-cce : Text =
    "tls13 label-cce=" & x-hex (tls-t2b "derived" 0 7 [])

  test-label-ascii : Text =
    "tls13 label-ascii=" & x-hex (tls13-ascii "derived")

  test-sni : Text =
    "tls13 sni=" & x-hex (tls-text-to-bytes "example.com")

  test-early : Text =
    "tls13 early=" & x-hex tls13-early-secret

  test-derived : Text =
    "tls13 derived=" & x-hex tls13-derived

  test-handshake : Text =
    "tls13 handshake=" & x-hex (tls13-handshake-secret x-ecdhe)

  test-c-hs : Text =
    "tls13 c-hs=" & x-hex (tls13-client-hs-traffic (tls13-handshake-secret x-ecdhe) x-transcript)

  test-s-hs : Text =
    "tls13 s-hs=" & x-hex (tls13-server-hs-traffic (tls13-handshake-secret x-ecdhe) x-transcript)

Section: Traffic Keys

  x-s-hs : List Integer =
    tls13-server-hs-traffic (tls13-handshake-secret x-ecdhe) x-transcript

  test-key : Text =
    "tls13 s-key=" & x-hex (tls13-traffic-key x-s-hs)

  test-iv : Text =
    "tls13 s-iv=" & x-hex (tls13-traffic-iv x-s-hs)

Section: Long Expansion

  x-long : List Integer = tls13-expand-label tls13-early-secret "derived" (tls13-hash []) 64

  test-long : Text =
    "tls13 long len=" & show (list-length x-long) & " tailne=" & show (list-at x-long 0 /= list-at x-long 32)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni test-hash
    print-line-uni test-label-cce
    print-line-uni test-label-ascii
    print-line-uni test-sni
    print-line-uni test-early
    print-line-uni test-derived
    print-line-uni test-handshake
    print-line-uni test-c-hs
    print-line-uni test-s-hs
    print-line-uni test-key
    print-line-uni test-iv
    print-line-uni test-long
  end
